  "query_preview": "..\nKEY MESSAGE 1 DESCRIPTION...\n\nDry weather is expected today with lighter winds. Still, the\npressure gradient with support 20 to perhaps 25 MPH gusts at\ntimes. It will be cooler than yesterday, with highs mainly in\nthe 70s with low humidity. \n\nHigh pressure begins to move in from the west tonight which will\nmean decoupling in the low levels, especially western and\nnorthern zones. This will lead to chilly overnight lows, with\nreadings dipping into the mid to upper 30s in the northern\nzones, with 40s most elsewhere.\n\nHigh pressure pushes off to our east on Saturday with return\nflow allowing for southerly SFC winds and widespread 70s for\nhighs. High clouds may increase late in the day.\n\nKEY MESSAGE 2 DESCRIPTION...\n\nShowers are expected Saturday night and into Sunday, as a cold front \nmoves eastward across the area. Ahead of the front, the convective \nenvironment is meek, with conditional instability through much of \nthe thermal profile. Ensembles have been hinting at showers that \nproduce more meaningful rainfall Saturday night given that PWATs are \nin the 90-95th percentile there. Once these showers pass however, \nPWATs will drop significantly as the cold front pushes eastward. So \nany showers that develop along the passing cold front on Sunday \nshould be much drier and produce lesser rainfall amounts.  As it \nstands now, it is likely that most people see a little rain Saturday \nnight and into Sunday. Southern NH should see more rainfall than the \nrest of NH and western Maine.\n\nAfter the cold front passes, high pressure moves in and will allow \nfor fair and crisp fall weather on Monday and Tuesday. Highs look to \nbe in the lower 70s. \n\nWith high pressure overhead, Monday night also looks to be clear and \nfeature a period of calmer winds. This could allow for radiational \ncooling and cold low temperatures, with areas north of the mountains \npotentially breaking 40F Tuesday morning.",
